# tee — real-world examples

## Write stdout to a file and the screen at once

```sh
ls | tee listing.txt
```

## Append instead of overwrite

```sh
echo "run at $(date)" | tee -a run.log
```

## Fan a stream out to several files

```sh
cat template.conf | tee host1.conf host2.conf host3.conf
```

## Write a root-owned file from a non-root pipe

```sh
echo "net.ipv4.ip_forward=1" | sudo tee /etc/sysctl.d/99-forward.conf
```

## Watch a long job and keep a log

```sh
zypper dup 2>&1 | tee upgrade.log
```

## Flag reference

| Flag | What it does                          |
| ---- | ------------------------------------- |
| -a   | append to files instead of truncating |
| -i   | ignore interrupt signals              |

> `sudo tee` is the standard trick for redirecting into a root-owned file, since
> the `>` redirect is handled by your non-root shell before sudo runs.
